Reproducible simulation protocol. Specified lognormal individual expectation 10 mg/L and population SD 9 mg/L, with reference 1 mg/L. Uniforms use Mulberry32, seed 20261031. Each normal variate uses the cosine Box-Muller branch with 1-u for the radial uniform; the sine branch is discarded. Each individual consumes exactly two outputs. Four new independent model draws make each sample. There is no resampling from a finite observed dataset, balancing, rejection, forced mean or reuse of an old sample. The first sample is included among the 5,000, not added again.

```javascript
function simulate(repetitions = 5000, sampleSize = 4, seed = 20261031) {
  let state = seed >>> 0;
  function uniform() {
    state = (state + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0;
    let t = state;
    t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1);
    t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61);
    return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
  }
  const logVariance = Math.log1p(81 / 100);
  const m = Math.log(10) - logVariance / 2;
  const s = Math.sqrt(logVariance);
  const rows = [], means = [], counts = Array(40).fill(0);
  for (let r = 0; r < repetitions; r++) {
    const row = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < sampleSize; i++) {
      const z = Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(1 - uniform())) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * uniform());
      row.push(Math.exp(m + s * z));
    }
    const mean = row.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / sampleSize;
    if (!(mean >= 0 && mean <= 80)) throw new Error("Mean outside published bin domain");
    counts[Math.min(39, Math.floor(mean / 2))]++;
    rows.push(row); means.push(mean);
  }
  return { rows, means, counts, m, s };
}
```

The code is provenance for the published TriG counts, not executable lecture UI. Fixed PRNG output approximates the independent-draw mathematical model; it does not prove physical independence or describe field measurements.

Same lognormal population and Mulberry32/cosine Box-Muller algorithm as C13: individual mean 10 mg/L, SD 9 mg/L, reference 1 mg/L. Reset seed 20261031 for this separate comparison. Within this run one continuous stream supplies disjoint new samples for sizes 1, 4, 16 and 64 in that order, 5,000 samples each. The run does not reuse the C13 four-value collection. There are 425,000 individual draws, two uniforms per draw. Mathematical independence is a model assumption; the PRNG supplies reproducible approximation, not physical evidence.

```javascript
function comparison() {
  let state=20261031;
  function uniform(){state=(state+0x6d2b79f5)>>>0;let t=state;t=Math.imul(t^(t>>>15),t|1);t^=t+Math.imul(t^(t>>>7),t|61);return((t^(t>>>14))>>>0)/4294967296;}
  const v=Math.log1p(.81),m=Math.log(10)-v/2,s=Math.sqrt(v),groups=[];
  for(const n of [1,4,16,64]){
    const means=[],counts=Array(40).fill(0);let outside=0;
    for(let r=0;r<5000;r++){
      let sum=0;
      for(let i=0;i<n;i++)sum+=Math.exp(m+s*Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(1-uniform()))*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*uniform()));
      const mean=sum/n;means.push(mean);
      if(mean<40)counts[Math.floor(mean)]++;else outside++;
    }
    groups.push({n,means,counts,outside});
  }
  const standardized=groups[3].means.map(x=>8*(x-10)/9),zCounts=Array(24).fill(0);
  for(const z of standardized){if(z< -6||z>6)throw new Error("Standardized value outside bins");zCounts[Math.min(23,Math.floor((z+6)*2))]++;}
  return {groups,standardized,zCounts,m,s};
}
```

Raw-scale frequency polygons use heights count/5000 for width-one bins. The common display window is 0 to 40 mg/L. Outside counts are 80, 0, 0 and 0; those observations are retained by this protocol, not discarded from the simulation or removed from the density denominator. Vertices at bin midpoints join linearly, with the first/last bin height carried to the window boundary. Zero tail runs can be represented by their endpoints. These polygons are approximate displays, not fitted densities or exact probability integrals. The standardized histogram retains every size-64 mean in fixed width-0.5 bins from -6 to 6.

A new constructed five-reading sample, distinct from S15, has concentrations 9.4, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2 and 10.6 mg/L. For this hypothetical one-sample two-sided test, prescribe a normal CDF with mean 10 mg/L and SD 0.50 mg/L before seeing the sample, with iid sampling and significance level 0.05. Neither parameter is estimated from these five readings. Their matching sample mean is a coincidence of construction, not fitting; sample SD is about 0.447214 mg/L, not the specified population SD.

Compute normal CDF as 0.5*(1+erf((x-10)/(0.50*sqrt(2)))). Model values at the sorted readings are 0.11506967022170844, 0.3445782583896764, 0.5, 0.6554217416103236 and 0.8849303297782916. Empirical right values are 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 and 1; left limits are 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6 and 0.8. Compare both jump sides, not just absolute differences after each jump or a finite display grid. Upward maximum is at 10.2 mg/L and downward maximum at 9.8 mg/L, both 0.1445782583896764 to floating precision.

The exact n=5 continuous-null two-sided 5% cutoff is 0.5632751983660635, not the asymptotic 1.36/sqrt(n) approximation. For distances d greater than one half, the two one-sided crossing events are disjoint. The exact two-sided survival probability is twice the one-sided Smirnov tail: 2*d times the sum over integer k=0 through floor(n*(1-d)) of choose(n,k)*(d+k/n)^(k-1)*(1-d-k/n)^(n-k). Solving this finite expression for tail 0.05 yields the authored cutoff. This identity is used only for calibration at that cutoff, not for a p value at the smaller observed D.

```python
import math
def exact_large_distance_tail(d, n=5):
    if not 0.5 < d < 1:
        raise ValueError("This exact two-sided shortcut requires d above one half")
    return 2*d*sum(math.comb(n,k)*(d+k/n)**(k-1)*(1-d-k/n)**(n-k)
                   for k in range(math.floor(n*(1-d))+1))
lo, hi = 0.500000000001, 0.999999999999
for _ in range(70):
    mid = (lo+hi)/2
    if exact_large_distance_tail(mid) > 0.05:
        lo = mid
    else:
        hi = mid
```

The observed D is below the cutoff: do not reject this prespecified model at 5%. This neither estimates a probability that the null is true nor verifies normality from five values. No p value is claimed. Normal CDF coordinates are theoretical samples joined linearly for drawing; test calculations use analytic values, not the drawing. ECDF coordinates use a right-continuous step renderer. Separate ECDF/model figures retain identical axes because interpolation is a chart-level shared capability; do not apply step interpolation to the smooth model or linear interpolation to the ECDF. The display window is 9 to 11 mg/L, not the normal support. Gap-chart lines merely join five computed discrepancies; they are not CDFs or observations. For parameters estimated from these data, use appropriately calibrated procedures, such as an applicable Lilliefors correction or a parametric bootstrap with re-estimation in every replicate. Discrete, rounded, censored and dependent cases require their own justification.

S15 uses a separate illustrative iid normal population, mean 10 mg/L and population SD the square root of 0.10 (mg/L) squared. These constants are chosen to match the scale of the constructed readings; they are not known true population parameters inferred from five observations and normality is not established by those observations. This model replaces the preceding C13/C14 lognormal population, whose individual SD was 9 mg/L.

Mulberry32 seed 20261031, uncached cosine Box-Muller, two uniforms per draw. Draw 5,000 fresh five-value samples, 25,000 individuals, without resampling, rejection or forcing sample moments. Fixed width-0.1 bins cover 8.8 to 11.2 mg/L. All means are retained; no density renormalization. The theoretical individual density is a separate reference, not part of the observation counts. Its normal support extends outside this display window. Normality makes the mean exactly normal at size five in this illustration, not by a size-five CLT guarantee.

```javascript
function standardErrorSimulation() {
  let state=20261031;
  function uniform(){state=(state+0x6d2b79f5)>>>0;let t=state;t=Math.imul(t^(t>>>15),t|1);t^=t+Math.imul(t^(t>>>7),t|61);return((t^(t>>>14))>>>0)/4294967296;}
  const means=[],counts=Array(24).fill(0);
  for(let r=0;r<5000;r++){
    let sum=0;
    for(let i=0;i<5;i++)sum+=10+Math.sqrt(.1)*Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(1-uniform()))*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*uniform());
    const mean=sum/5;means.push(mean);
    if(mean<8.8||mean>11.2)throw new Error("Mean outside fixed bins");
    counts[Math.min(23,Math.floor((mean-8.8)/.1))]++;
  }
  return {means,counts};
}
```

This authored provenance is not executable lecture UI. Pseudorandom draws approximate an independent sampling model and do not prove physical independence. The constant-bias example on S15d is a separate hypothetical scenario, not a diagnosis of the five readings. With correlated observations, the variance of a mean is the sum of all pairwise covariances divided by sample size squared; off-diagonal covariance terms need not vanish. General measurement uncertainty may contain contributions beyond repeatability.

Continue S15's specified iid normal teaching model, mean 10 mg/L and variance 0.10 (mg/L) squared. Replay exactly its seed and draws, not an independent new collection: 5,000 fresh five-value samples, Mulberry32 seed 20261031, uncached cosine Box-Muller, two uniforms per draw. The displayed five bars are the first five variances in order, not selected extrema or a histogram. They are not the variance of S15's constructed symmetric observations. The chi-square curve is theoretical, not fitted to these five bars.

```javascript
function varianceSimulation() {
  let state=20261031;
  function uniform(){state=(state+0x6d2b79f5)>>>0;let t=state;t=Math.imul(t^(t>>>15),t|1);t^=t+Math.imul(t^(t>>>7),t|61);return((t^(t>>>14))>>>0)/4294967296;}
  const means=[],variances=[];
  for(let r=0;r<5000;r++){
    const row=[];
    for(let i=0;i<5;i++)row.push(10+Math.sqrt(.1)*Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(1-uniform()))*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*uniform()));
    const mean=row.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/5;
    means.push(mean);variances.push(row.reduce((a,x)=>a+(x-mean)**2,0)/4);
  }
  return {means,variances};
}
```

All line coordinates are theoretical density values, rounded to twelve decimal places and joined linearly, not raw observations or fitted curves. Chi-square with four degrees of freedom has density x exp(-x/2)/4 for nonnegative dimensionless x. Student-t with nu degrees of freedom has density Gamma((nu+1)/2)/(sqrt(nu*pi)*Gamma(nu/2)) times (1+x*x/nu)^(-(nu+1)/2). The normal reference is reused from C14. Plot windows do not bound support or imply renormalization. T curves use nu 2, 4, 10 and 30 on identical axes.

For the optional interval, use the S15 constructed observations, not a simulated sample: mean 10, sample variance 0.10, estimated SE sqrt(0.10/5). The Student-t cumulative-probability-0.975 quantile for four degrees of freedom is 2.7764451051977987; margin 0.39264863229551217 mg/L and endpoints 9.607351367704488 and 10.392648632295512 mg/L. Quantiles refer to the full theoretical distribution, not integrals of the piecewise-linear drawing. Under iid normal sampling the interval procedure has exact 95% coverage. This does not assign 95% probability to the fixed unknown population mean after observing this particular interval; it is not an individual-reading prediction interval and does not correct systematic bias.
